You have the perfect test scenario mapped out. Your logic for the glcc keys is 100% correct:
 * **Row 1 Target:** 32121USD1122505001 for 2025-12-02
 * **Row 2 Target:** 32121USD1208505003 for 2025-12-02
Since you want to start with a clean test, we first need to inject these baseline records into your Data Lake so that the CSV upload actually has something to update!
Here is the exact beeline command to insert this baseline data into your Data Lake. I gave them starting balances of 50000.00 and 25000.00 so that you can easily see the numbers change when your Java application processes the CSV.
### Step 1: Insert the Baseline Data into HDFS
Run this exact command in your terminal:
```bash
kubectl exec -it deploy/spark-thrift -n asd --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf -- /opt/spark/bin/beeline -u jdbc:hive2://localhost:10000/fincoredatalakejournal -e "INSERT INTO gl_balance (glcc, closing_balance, INR_BALANCE, BALANCE_DATE) VALUES ('32121USD1122505001', 50000.0000, 4150000.0000, '2025-12-02'), ('32121USD1208505003', 25000.0000, 2075000.0000, '2025-12-02');"

```
*(Note: Depending on your Spark setup, Delta Lake might take a few seconds to commit the insert).*
### Step 2: Verify the Baseline Data is there
Just to be absolutely sure the insert worked before you upload the CSV:
```bash
kubectl exec -it deploy/spark-thrift -n asd --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf -- /opt/spark/bin/beeline -u jdbc:hive2://localhost:10000/fincoredatalakejournal -e "SELECT glcc, closing_balance, BALANCE_DATE FROM gl_balance WHERE BALANCE_DATE = '2025-12-02';"

```
### Step 3: Run Your CSV Update
Once you see the 50000 and 25000 balances sitting in the Data Lake, go to your frontend, upload your exact CSV file:
```csv
01,02122025,2025,09,32121,USD,1122505001,1000,Cr,Normal,12345679,B,C,D
01,02122025,2025,09,32121,USD,1208505003,1000,Dr,ewreew,12345678,B,C,D

```
Approve the batch, watch the Spring Boot logs for that beautiful HDFS_SYNC_SUCCESS message, and run the SELECT query from Step 2 again to watch the balances perfectly update!
